yes we've airports are a high fixed cost  business you  even if we only have one passenger we  still need to keep the whole airfield  safe and  secure and all the equipment moving and  we've worked  so hard over the last 12 months to get  our costs down by about a third  but of course there's only so much that  we can do particularly when the  government isn't giving us any relief on  business rates which is a  a big chunk of our cost base but we are  now starting to see signs that  passengers are coming back  people want to be able to travel and as  soon as we can start to get borders open  up again  and start to see doubly vaccinated  passengers from the eu  and the u.s traveling again we will  start to see our  uh our business recovering and with it  the uk economy  to what extent are you being hurt by  workers having to self-isolate right now  i mean it's been widely reported over  the weekend that there was a lot of  chaos on the ground at heathrow  we've had a couple of incidents where a  lot of people have been pinged  all at the same time which is very hard  to manage even with the daily contact  testing  system which we do have in place here  but it's a good system and it means that  we can keep the airport flowing and keep  getting passengers and  the uk's exports on their way but the  the main thing we need to do  is to start looking to the future get  doubly vaccinated passengers traveling  and also make it easier with  pcr tests uh getting rid of pcr tests  for people coming from low risk  countries  and replacing them with lateral flow  tests just to cut the cost  and the hassle that comes with travel  today  sorry just to go back to what happened  what was wrong at the weekend what what  specifically went wrong  so we had an issue in border force for a  couple of hours  when we had a combination of a lot of  officers being pinged and not being able  to uh man the desks  and at the same time the e-gates which a  lot of passengers traveled through  also went down and that led to cues of a  couple of hours for a short period  but i'm in terminal five at the moment  and speaking to passengers  they are amazed at how quickly they are  getting through immigration in just a  couple of minutes  so it shows this is uh what happened at  the weekend was the unusual  circumstance usually people get through  very quickly  you've reopened terminal 3 11 days ago  how has that gone thus far  it's gone really well and it's good to  have a bit more capacity  for passengers in particular one of the  consequences of all the checks that have  to be put in place is that it takes a  little bit longer to check in now  than it would normally take it can take  10 or 15 minutes for  the check-in agents to make sure that  you've got all the paperwork you'll need  to get into the country you're going to  and that means we just need a little bit  more space  in check-in and that's why we've opened  terminal 3 earlier  but we're also anticipating that the  demand will come back in the second half  of this year  and we want to get the whole airport up  and running again so we can meet that  demand and help to get britain traveling  again  and get the economy off the ground and  it's shocking today  seeing how low our cargo volumes have  fallen  and you know that we are the biggest uk  port we carry 40  of uk exports and yet our volumes of  cargo are down by 20  when countries like germany and the  netherlands are up by nearly 10 percent  and that shows that their more  progressive approach to opening borders  is helping their economy at the expense  of ours  and it's a bit like we've won the race  for vaccination and yet we don't even  get into the medals because we're not  prepared to  follow up and get the benefits of the  vaccination program  i mean obviously a lot of cargo uh  normally travels  in the in the hold along with uh  passenger flights to what extent are you  seeing more airlines running cargo only  flights  we've seen a big uplift in past in cargo  only flights but the great thing about  cargo that goes in the belly of  passenger planes is that those planes  are traveling  every single day of the week sometimes  many times a day  and that means that if you're a uk  exporter trying to get your goods to  market  it's cheap and quick to get them to any  part of the world  if you're depending on a cargo only  flight that plane might only be going  once a week  which adds to the supply chain time and  adds to the cost of getting it to market  one of the great advantages that uk  businesses have is the connectivity from  heathrow which normally  is the best of any airport in the world  means they can get their goods and their  people  anywhere they want to in the world  within 24 hours  and we have lost that advantage because  of these restrictions on passengers  flying we can get it back we just need  to be more optimistic  and and to open up to doubly vaccinated  passengers from more countries  and reduce the friction of travel by  getting rid of these  expensive pcr tests from low-risk  countries  now that free shopping is now a thing of  the past how concerned are you that more  retailers are going to follow the lead  of dixons who are now of course  closing all their airport stores well  yes it's a real shame to see dickson's  uh move out i'm very pleased that w h  smith is stepping in to take their place  and we do have a lot of demand from  retailers to have stores  in the airport it's an important part of  the passenger journey people look  forward to starting their holiday or  their trip  once they get to the airport and either  to sock up on things that they haven't  been able to  uh to buy before or to treat themselves  so we currently have about 75 percent of  all of our stores and restaurants open  and we'll be looking to get that up to a  hundred percent as soon as we can  all right john holland good to talk to  you as ever thanks for joining me this  morning
 

